#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:5
#: e28898d1375a400bbb853619823f138f
msgid "Log Analysis Case Study: Fly-by-Wire"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:7
#: acf88314ebb6408fbbec5f92ff1f21c9
msgid "Analyzing flight logs can help you diagnose problems with your missions. This case study brings you an example of a flight where, by analyzing the log data, our resident expert, Andrew Tridgell, suggested increasing the waypoint radius to correct the plane's failure to complete a neat navigation path. The original forum posts can be found \\ `**here** <https://diydrones.com/forum/topics/arduplane-2-73-released?commentId=705844%3AComment%3A1265467>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:14
#: e4edc9aeb9f640fe97a11f5e90e85c60
msgid "DIY Drones member Iskess posted these logs of his X8 plane using APM code 2.73. He described an issue with auto take off where his plane wasn't achieving enough bank angle in FBW (only about 30 degrees) but used the full 60 degrees perfectly in the auto modes. Iskess notes that the flight starts off with a couple laps around a course of tightly set waypoints, and FBW-B doesn't start until 77.2% through the log."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:21
#: c4e6025846544514b94a8db99389f8bd
msgid "5/23/2013 tlog file (1.9 MB) - Download from original blog post \\ `**here** <https://diydrones.com/forum/topics/arduplane-2-73-released?commentId=705844%3AComment%3A1265467>`__. - `X8 Parameters spreadsheet file download (14 KB) <https://download.ardupilot.org/downloads/wiki/other_files/X8Parameters.xlsx>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:24
#: 7c0dd3bab4434388b788e99037e7d45e
msgid "By analyzing this data using \\ **`Mavgraph <http://qgroundcontrol.org/mavlink/mavproxy_startpage#mavgraphpy>`__**, Tridge was able to provide the following insight."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:28
#: e7a0f7a6ac8c42fda6cf2ef6de4d8af4
msgid "***May 29, 2013 DIYDrones; posted to Plane 2.x Software forum:***"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:30
#: 3870b4a09483414281a7748caba6851b
msgid "\"Let's look at the 2nd auto mission first:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:35
#: a634f7f8df394c689de91f3e688227a6
msgid "It clearly could do with some improvement. Let's see if we can find out what's wrong."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:38
#: 493b8d6a6ae2456d8aa81e79f044b46b
msgid "First off, let's graph the demanded roll versus the actual roll:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:43
#: 16c126bcd38848f68b493aa7adf34bee
msgid "That looks good! The plane is achieving the desired roll quickly and accurately. Now let's check the pitch:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:49
#: 4452d3a04d5b4f3e9c88e277736b0707
msgid "That's not as good, though its only off by a few degrees. The PTCH2SRV\\_\\* parameters could do with some improvement. I'd add a bit of I term to that (increase :ref:`PTCH_RATE_I<PTCH_RATE_I>` to 0.1), and also increase :ref:`PTCH_RATE_IMAX<PTCH_RATE_IMAX>` to around 1000."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:54
#: f5128f51e5394cdd9b5fdc681a7c9090
msgid "That isn't the cause of the navigation problems though. Let's look more carefully at the key nav parameters you have:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:57
#: 7eeafcb0a62f4c93b59594ce2334dce8
msgid ":ref:`WP_RADIUS<WP_RADIUS>`: 8"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:58
#: 2b12f332cfa740c79eebe14f1073f8ef
msgid ":ref:`NAVL1_PERIOD<NAVL1_PERIOD>`: 18"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:59
#: 306bae1bf17d4412a31927cd07f17b5a
msgid ":ref:`NAVL1_DAMPING<NAVL1_DAMPING>`: 0.8"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:60
#: 35921d79dfdb402f93389f0768c74f89
msgid ":ref:`ROLL_LIMIT_DEG<ROLL_LIMIT_DEG>`: 60"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:62
#: e89e59af05e64a7ea6306c4076df7f93
msgid "The one that is furthest off for this sort of track is WP_RADIUS. You are flying at about 18 m/s on average, and your uBlox GPS will have a lag of around 0.6 seconds which is about 11 m of flight distance, which means you are not turning until you are past the waypoint. If that is what you want then that's fine, but if you want the plane to turn neatly onto the next track then you'll need to turn earlier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:69
#: 1b202ffdd3f24fadb71af7d08a881d22
msgid "Your :ref:`ROLL_LIMIT_DEG<ROLL_LIMIT_DEG>` of 60 degrees at 18 m/s corresponds to a turn radius of 19.1 m. The plane also takes a second or two to change attitude. (The X8 is not a fast response plane! It's more like flying a manta ray). If we add 1 second of flight time for the attitude change plus the GPS lag plus the turn radius, then you'd need to turn about 48 m before a 90 degree turn to end up neatly on track. (That is very rough of course, but won't be too far off). So :ref:`WP_RADIUS<WP_RADIUS>` of 48 or so will produce a much neater track."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:78
#: 9baee26c172b4e0b98e0fcb1bb57a56b
msgid "I also think you could lower :ref:`NAVL1_PERIOD<NAVL1_PERIOD>` a bit more. I don't see any weaving there, just xtrack changes, so I'd try 15 and see if it weaves. You may even find 13 works well. You'll need to experiment as the right value is very airframe dependent (and even depends on the type of servo you have!). You may also like to increase ROLL_LIMIT_DEG to 65 for a bit faster turns."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:85
#: fcf8b16a562f4449a0787573d6706e86
msgid "Finally let's look at your altitude handling:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:90
#: 1b9a19ce6d504d3dbb663e84b257a027
msgid "The most noticeable thing is that you started the mission well above the mission altitude of 61 m. It took it four waypoints just to get down to the target altitude. That makes using the log for nav tuning harder, as the plane was trying to descend while navigating. Once it reached the target altitude it held it OK, varying by about 10 m. That isn't fantastic, but tuning the X8 for good altitude handling in turns is very hard with the current controller in APM. You'll need the new TECS controller Paul is working on to get really good results. So for now I wouldn't suggest changing anything there."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:100
#: eb7a7fa7edd742ae97ecc90283dc1fca
msgid "Regarding FWB-B, that can be a little tricky to analyze, and is one of the reasons I added ELEVON_OUTPUT, as it makes analyzing demanded roll a lot easier! As you'll see below, working out the demanded roll for elevons is somewhat tricky."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:105
#: 4f664cfb9ce24ab6896e8f8d10203b7c
msgid "First we should grab the demanded and achieved roll when in FBW-B:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:110
#: 4223b6f92e9a4e58aa965ca70e4b075e
msgid "You can see that the X8 is achieving the roll that is being asked for fairly well. The roll is a bit jittery in level flight (maybe elevons a bit too loose? or wind effects? hard to say), but it does generally do what is being asked of it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:115
#: ac15666a96c543ad90d0665044aadcfb
msgid "Now let's look at what your RC input was against the demanded roll. The demanded roll is in blue, and your RC inputs are in green and red."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:121
#: 8c69f6c719f44755a08f9ec040be0677
msgid "The RC limits you had set were:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:123
#: 1f5ee9a5082e42adae3f7100e6816e1c
msgid ":ref:`RC1_MAX<RC1_MAX>`         1936"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:124
#: 668551e0c5414213824736385dd8b868
msgid ":ref:`RC1_MIN<RC1_MIN>`         1012"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:125
#: 15387e2b1f9946e79e636446661993b3
msgid ":ref:`RC2_MAX<RC2_MAX>`         1927"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:126
#: 81c854d0faca488baf97071a14659ccb
msgid ":ref:`RC2_MIN<RC2_MIN>`         986"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:127
#: fc7e984298cc495c95d765453e20afd1
msgid ":ref:`ROLL_LIMIT_DEG<ROLL_LIMIT_DEG>`  60"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:129
#: 751e1d24008146bd87b24a1782b30903
msgid "We can also see from this graph that you had a bit of transmitter trim. The part of the graph where the nav_roll is zero shows your RC input was around 1420 for each channel. That is important."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:133
#: f89642cf2ed44d099751a667ad454704
msgid "Let's zoom in a bit on the part of the FBWB flight where you were trying to turn hard right:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:139
#: 4cffd6cbaebe4b9eb9db35159461973c
msgid "You can see that your transmitter was not outputting values anywhere near the limits that had been setup in radio calibration. The average of the two channels was a bit above 1700, which is 320 above trim, and 227 below the RC1_MAX."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:144
#: 2d3e22b6a69b4ebd8b46f1151a845445
msgid "So now we know its output is 320 PWM above trim, the full range above trim is 1420 to 1927, and the roll limit is 60 degrees. It is a simple linear proportion to get demanded roll, so that corresponds to 320/(1927-1420)\\*60 degrees of demanded roll, which is 37.8 degrees. That matches the nav_roll the plane got and what it ended up flying."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:150
#: a8b9467f76ec4548bc8c99562ef9ce22
msgid "Note that I've done a few shortcuts in the above. The full calculation for demanded roll with elevons is a bit messier. See the code in \\ `radio.cpp <https://github.com/tridge/ardupilot/blob/master/ArduPlane/radio.cpp#L129>`__ in the ``read_radio()`` function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:155
#: 64b29568646e4ec7804b79fe5e4097c6
msgid "(If you read that code you may understand why I don't like the ELEVON_MIXING option and much prefer the simpler ELEVON_OUTPUT code!)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:158
#: 98c5da1c2bcd41cb94b585f91ee568cc
msgid "To answer your question, your transmitter is giving RC outputs which are much less than the :ref:`RC1_MAX<RC1_MAX>` and :ref:`RC1_MIN<RC1_MIN>` values you have in the autopilot (presumably from when you did radio calibration). That is why it isn't rolling over as much as you want."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/case-study-fly-by-wire.rst:163
#: d7877d09b2844a329c4b3618d07fad1c
msgid "Cheers, Tridge\""
msgstr ""
